#passage(("What is there to learn from pre-Islamic Arabia? For me personally, this is one of the reasons I'm so interested: I come across these names in the [Seerah] and I had no idea about who these people were. The prophet, peace be upon him, in [Bukhari] tells [Adi bin] Hatim, if you live long enough, you will live to see a woman travel from [Hira] to the [Kaaba] to circumambulate it and not fear anyone but Allah. [Adi] said, \"So I said in my heart, but what about the raiders that had spread across the land?\" This is a fulfilled prophecy, by the way. But why is it significant?", "When [Umayyah] was killed in the battle of Badr, why was [Abdur Rahman] so upset? Isn't it strange that [Abdur Rahman] wanted this major enemy of Islam to stay alive? Before the prophet, peace be upon him, went to hijra, he approached Benu Amir with his message, but it didn't work out. Most of us have never even heard of Benu [Amir]. And when we just come across the name of the tribe in a page, it just means nothing to you. It's just a name. But when you know the history of Benu [Amir], then you put your hands on your head in shock.", "For many people, it would simply be a matter of curiosity about this era that not so many people know much about. The most famous thing about Jahalia is that people worship multiple gods, and this was something that ended when Islam emerged. For some, it could be that learning about Jahalyah played a role in their appreciation for Islam, since Islam not only influenced matters to do with paganism alone, but it had a major effect on many aspects of the culture. Regardless of your reasons, you should be aware that Jahalia history is fascinating, especially matters that have to do with the wars between the tribes.",), "Farid Responds, “I didn't understand this about the Seerah until...”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=NMNcvv38Bns", ((0, "0:00–0:35"), (44, "0:44–1:41"), (101, "1:41–2:22"),))
// U FR023:1-46 | Farid Responds | 7
#passage(("I feel this is the right time to shed light on the religion and the beliefs of the people of the peninsula. I don't want to go into much detail about the religions of the northern kingdoms yet, I'm referring to the Ghassanids and Lakhmids, since they were both different Christian sects. I want to mainly focus on the beliefs and practices of the pagan Arabs themselves.", "As we know, the pagans worshipped idols, statues that they built with their own hands. But it is important to not have a simplistic view of the pagans. They didn't believe that their statues created them or anything like that. They believed that their idols were representatives.", "Ibn Abbas reports in Sahih al-Bukhari some of the background that led to the emergence of shirk or polytheism in pre-Islamic Arabia. He said, \"All the idols which were worshipped by the people of Noah were worshipped by the Arabs later on. As for the idol Wadd, it was worshipped by the tribe of Kalb at Dumat al-Jandal. Suwa' was the idol of the tribe of Hudhayl. Yaghuth was worshipped by the tribe of Murad and then by Banu Ghutayf at al-Jawf near Saba. Ya'uq was the idol of Hamdan, and Nasr was the idol of Himyar, the branch of Dhu al-Kila'. The names of the idols formerly belonged to some pious men of the people of Noah. And when they died, Satan inspired their people to prepare and place idols at the places where they used to sit and to call those idols by their names. The people did so, but the idols were not worshipped till those people who initiated them had died and the origin of the idols had become obscure. Whereupon people began worshipping them.\"", "If you notice what Ibn Abbas said, he mentioned that Yaghuth was first worshipped by the tribe of Murad and then it was worshipped by the tribe of Banu Ghatafan. Muhammad bin Habib al-Baghdadi, who's an early historian, explains that Yaghuth was an idol that was worshipped by Madhhij as a whole, which includes multiple tribes, and it was specifically among the tribe of An'um. However, the tribe of Ghatafan fought them over it, and then they escaped with the idol to Najran among Banu al-Harith bin Ka'b. In the reports of Tabari, Yaghuth would once be shifted over from tribe to tribe that would continuously be fighting over it. One of the battles ended in a lot of bloodshed, and it occurred during the time of the Battle of Badr. It was known as the Day of al-Razzam.", "The shapes of these idols came in different forms. Wadd was in the form of a man. Suwa' was in the form of a woman, and al-Lat was a white cube with carvings. By the way, these were the major idols. There were smaller idols that people kept in their homes as well, and apparently it was quite common. Pagans from around the peninsula would even purchase idols from the people of Mecca.", "In another report, Ibn Hisham narrates from the people of knowledge of his time that Amr bin Luhai left Mecca towards Sham, and when he was in al-Balqa, which is modern-day Jordan, he met some Amalekites that were worshipping idols, and he asked them, \"What are these idols that you're worshipping?\" So they said, \"These are idols that we worship. If we ask for rain, then rain comes down for us. And if we ask for victory, then victory comes to us.\" So he said, \"Do you mind giving me an idol to take to the land of the Arabs for them to worship?\" So they gave him an idol named Hubal. So he brought it to Mecca and told the people to worship and glorify it. Amr bin Luhai is the father of Khuza'a, which means that he passed away 300 years before Islam. This means that Hubal was brought to Mecca relatively late.", "For a comprehensive list of the idols, there are two recommended works that I would suggest. The first is Kitab al-Asnam by Ibn al-Kalbi, and even though he's not generally a reliable historian, there's little reason for us to assume that the information contained within are fabrications. And the second is a contemporary work called Mu'jam Alihat al-Arab Qabl al-Islam, which is a modern work, and it gets information from various sources.",), "Farid Responds, “The Religion and Superstitions of pre-Islamic Arabs”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=66UjYhSopgM", ((5, "0:05–0:41"), (41, "0:41–1:05"), (69, "1:09–2:25"), (148, "2:28–3:25"), (208, "3:28–3:55"), (235, "3:55–4:57"), (297, "4:57–5:32"),))
#author("The Muslim Lantern")
// U ML382:1-38 | The Muslim Lantern | 2
#passage(("They knew about the idea of God, but they had what we call polytheism as well, like worshipping idols and all of that. But they did believe there is something that created the universe. It depends what you mean by \"Allah\". If you mean Allahﷻ, which means God in the Arabic language: الإله (the God), then they used that term, because it's an Arabic term. So they used it, but they used it for false deities and they used it for The Creator as well. So they believed that there is a Creator, The Creator that created everything.", "They believed in the One True God, not in all His Attributes, because it was not revealed to them. But they had a general idea of The Creator, The One True Creator. And then they associated partners with that One True Creator. So they had remnants of Abraham's teachings.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Insincere Christian Embarrasses Himself! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=r8uum7KFEnY", ((16, "0:16–0:46"), (84, "1:24–1:36"),))
#author("Farid Responds")
// U FR023:47-200 | Farid Responds | 16
#passage(("In terms of how the Arabs view their idols, we find the Quran quoting them in Surat al-Zumar saying, \"We worship them only so they may bring us closer to Allah.\" In other words, in many cases, these deities weren't independent gods, but rather they were intermediaries. However, they still offered them worship. Now, keep in mind that, unlike many people today, the Arabs at the time understood the concept of worship. People today think that worship is specifically directed at a deity, as in, if you perform an act of worship towards a human, it isn't counted as worship, since that human is not a deity. Now, this is a baseless claim that conflicts with the dictionary definition, which is: ibadah, worship, is the height of humility and subservience.", "What were the beliefs and practices of the Arabs towards these idols? Well, Hubal specifically would be approached for knowledge of the unseen. Al-Azraqi reported from Ibn Ishaq that at Hubal there would be seven cups. Each of these cups would have a label: a cup for compensations, which is used for whenever a compensation needs to be paid and there's a difference among those that were trying to decide on the compensation. And there would be a cup in matters that they differed in, in which the answer would be a yes or no, in order for them to decide matters in which they differed in. Ibn Ishaq says, if they wish to circumcise a child or get married or bury the dead, or if they doubted someone's lineage, they would go to Hubal with 100 dirhams and slaughtered meat, and they would give it to the custodian of the cups. Then they'd bring forward the person that they came for, and then they would say, \"Oh our god, this is the person we wanted for him this and that, so bring out the truth concerning him.\" Then they would say to the custodian of the cups to draw the arrow. So if the arrow came out with the words, he's from you, then he would be accepted as someone from them. And if it came out that he's from other than you, they would consider him to be an ally. If it came out as attached, meaning [mulsaq], then they would consider him to be a [mulsaq], but without attributing to him a genealogy or an alliance. By the way, a bit of a side note, they used cups for gambling as well.", "A fascinating example of shirk is mentioned in Sirat Ibn Ishaq when speaking of the tribe of Khawlan and their idol that is referred to as Amm Anis, or in some reports Ummyanis. Ibn Ishaq mentions they used to divide their crops and cattle between it and Allah. And if any of Allah's portion, which they had earmarked for him, came into Amm Anis's portion, they'd leave it for him. But if any of Amm Anis's portion went into Allah's portion, they would return it to him. And this wouldn't be solely for animals, but it would also include crops. We find in Surat Al-An'am that the pagans would set aside for Allah a share of the crops and the cattle he created, saying, \"This portion is for Allah,\" and so they claim, \"and this one is for our associate gods.\" So in this example here, the picture is getting clearer. Some of the polytheists even seem to favor their demigods over Allah Subhana Wa Ta'ala in their actions.", "Ibn Qayyim quotes a report about the idol Amm Anis, in which, when the tribe of Khawlan came to the prophet, peace be upon him, to pledge their allegiance to him, he asked them about their idol. So they said, Allah Subhana Wa Ta'ala had substituted them in the place of their former, that which you, ya Muhammad, brought to us. And there are some old men and women that are still holding on to him, but if we return, then we will surely destroy him, for He was a trial. So the messenger, peace be upon him, said, \"What was the greatest trial that you had seen from him?\" And they said, \"We saw a time in which we ate leftover bones. So we brought together all that we could and brought with it a hundred bulls, then slaughtered it to [Amm Anis] as a means to get closer to him in a single day. And we left the slaughtered bulls for the beasts. And we are in greater need for them than the beasts. And when the rain came down upon us, and we saw the grass grow high enough to cover a man.\"", "Now, by the way, I'm personally haven't found an authentic path for this report, but what it does seem to do is reflect the understanding of the people of the time. People, they would seek the help of idols and ask them for rain. And in some cases, it indeed rained, naturally, which caused people to believe that the rain is from the idols, and the idols had the ability to grant them blessings and provisions. Now, rain meant life or death for the Arabs. In occasions like the one I mentioned, they would seek their idols to keep them alive instead of asking Allah Subhana Wa Ta'ala.", "Another interesting form of sacrifice can be found in the Quran. We find a verse stating that Allah didn't institute a bahira, a sa'iba, or a ham. Now, we come across these words in the Quran, but the meaning are unknown to most Arabs today, since we're unfamiliar with the traditions and or the practices of the time. We also find a narration by the prophet, peace be upon him, speaking of the second of these, the Sa'ibah. He says that he was shown Amr ibn Luhayy in hellfire dragging his intestines. And he was the first to... and then he says in Arabic, \"Sayyab as-Sawa'ib,\" which translates to, \"He was the first to let the camels loose.\" So, what is exactly meant here by letting the camels loose? Ibn Hisham explains that a person would vow that if he was cured from a sickness or if he was given something that he asked for, then he would release one of his camels in the name of the gods. And when he released a camel, it would just be released and no one would be allowed to benefit from it. In other words, one would not be able to make use of the fur of the camel. One would not be able to milk the camel. One would not be able to eat the camel. And that is one form of giving thanks to the gods, from among the people of Jahiliyyah; that's what they would do.", "As for al-Bahirah, Abu Hilal al-Askari explains in his book Kitab al-Awail that if a she-camel gave birth five times, they would cut its ear as a sign. They would do this if the fifth is a female. And they would then forbid their own women from eating its meat or from drinking its milk. However, if the fifth was a male, they would sacrifice it to their gods. The men would eat the camel alone, not allowing the women to eat it either. Now, this is similar to the previous example, in which the polytheists would be receiving something from Allah Subhana Wa Ta'ala, but they would be thanking their idols instead. In this case, their rizq came in the form of a she-camel that had given birth five different times. It is important to keep in mind that it's not something that occurs frequently. It would take like 20 years for a camel to give birth five times. And when that finally happens, they would often be sacrificing the male camel to one of their gods. It should be noted that one of the reasons that this seems to be a sacrifice is because this is not something that someone would typically do. They would not typically slaughter a young camel, due to issues with scarcity, and because one would naturally rather let them grow to its full size before slaughtering it.", "Another example of something that is given to the gods is what is called al-wasilah. Said ibn Musayyib mentions in Sahih Bukhari that the wasilah is the she-camel that gives birth to a female first, then she gives birth to another female after, without any males in between. The mother is then referred to as the wasilah, and she is left alone and freed in the name of the gods. Said then continues and he defines what is known as al-ham. And that is the male camel that has had intercourse with multiple female camels, impregnating them. And after he has completed what was assigned to him, he is let go free for the sake of the gods. Abu Hilal al-Askari adds that this would occur after 10 she-camels gave birth. He is set loose and his back is protected so that nobody rides him, and he is allowed to roam freely, and nobody prevents him from drinking or from grazing. So, all of these terms describing sacrifices of camels in appreciation of the idols, which of course is polytheism, and they did accept this to be polytheism.", "By the way, the custodians of the idols were those that would benefit most from the idol worship in the societies, since offerings that are made toward the idols would be given to them. Each of the major idols had custodians from different tribes. So, for example, Wadd was under the care of Banu Amr. Dhul Khalasa was under the care of Banu Hilal, and so on. And naturally, they were seen as the most honorable subtribes in the region for their station.", "Now, in regards to superstitions, there was a large variety of beliefs that existed at the time. One example is a common word that was used: tatayyur, which has to do with the movement of the birds. If someone starts off a journey and notices birds flying towards the right, they would consider it to be a sign for the journey. If the birds flew towards the left, they would consider it a bad sign. They would even return home. However, different Arabs interpreted these movements in different ways. So, for some, if the birds were flying from the left to the right, it would be a bad sign. However, the concept is the same. The Arabs would use the same word to describe pretty much anything that was considered to be a bad sign. So, for example, if they went to the marketplace and saw a person with one eye, or if they saw an animal that only had one eye at the edge of the marketplace, they would take it as a bad sign for business. This would all fall under [tiyarah] or [tatayyur].", "And this sort of thing wasn't specific to animals or humans. It would include actions as well, including something as random as sneezing. If someone sneezed, people would sometimes see it as a sign of bad things to come. They would say, \"I ask God to make the ill omen of your sneeze be upon you and not me.\" Now, this would also extend to the days of the week. Every Wednesday, if it was on the fourth day of the month, they considered to be a day of bad omens. Or the month of Shawwal as a whole, it was seen as a month that one should not get married in.", "There were some strange beliefs that were widespread for unknown reasons. For example, there was a plant, it's called the retama. It's a white broom, that's what it's referred to as, and travelers would go up to it and tie a knot around it before going ahead on their journey. Upon returning to their home, if the knot is still there, they would assume that their wives have been faithful to them. But if it was no longer there, they would assume that their wives cheated on them. They also believed that turning around to look back at your town while going on a journey will lead to misfortune. If someone was [about] to enter a town that's had a widespread disease, or if it had the jinn that they feared, they would be at the outskirts of the town and they would start braying like a donkey. And they would hang a rabbit's foot as a form of protection. If someone was lost, they would shout the name of the person into an old well three times. If they heard a sound from within, they would assume that the person is still alive. But if they heard no sound, they would assume the person is dead. If someone bought a house, then they would make a sacrifice by slaughtering for the jinn as a form of protection. If someone had a thousand camels, they would fear getting the evil eye. So in order to counter it, they would pop the eye off one of their camels.", "And one of the stranger beliefs is that if someone gets rabies through a dog's bite, then the cure for it would be the blood of a king. They would extract the king's blood from his finger, mix it with water, and have the patient drink it to be healed. Another weird belief among pre-Islamic Arabs is that if there were two tribes that were about to go to war, and that some wished to prevent, they would have women go in between both tribes and to urinate in order to extinguish the flames of war. It's a very strange metaphor that they put into practice to turn into some sort of reality. It's somehow supposed to lead to peace.", "One odd aspect regarding the beliefs of the Arabs was that they did not believe in life after death, due to the lack of proof for life after death in their eyes. However, it does seem like they were very superstitious people that built their beliefs on coincidences and signs, seeking any sign as a good omen or bad one before making decisions, causing them to attribute to their idols that which coincidentally matched any good fortune that they sought.", "I feel the need to share a few words on the topic of al-Hums, since it is something that is tied to religion and gives you a better understanding of Quraysh and their status among the people. The Hums refers to Quraysh, Kinana, the whole of Kinana of which Quraysh is a part of, and Banu Amir bin Sa'sa'a. And it didn't include Banu Bakr bin Abd Manat from within Kinana, due to their bad blood with Quraysh. The Hums made religious rulings for themselves, since they considered themselves to be superior to other Arabs, as they were custodians of the Kaaba. They wouldn't churn butter or render ghee while in a state of ihram. Nor would they enter a tent of hair or seek shade except for if it was a leather tent. If someone wasn't from the Hums, they would forbid that person from bringing their own food as pilgrims. If they circumambulated around the Kaaba, they need to wear clothes that were made by the Hums. If they couldn't find the Hums clothing, they would have to circumambulate the Kaaba naked. If they circumambulated with their own clothes, then they would [have] to throw their own clothes away when they are done.", "Anyway, the people had a general respect for Quraysh and they abided by these rules. And there was a type of segregation that occurred during Hajj, in which those that were not from the Hums, they would go to Arafah in order to worship, while the Hums would go to Muzdalifah. And [Jubayr] bin Mut'im narrates, as we find in Sahih Muslim, he says, \"I lost my camel, so I went to look for it on the day of Arafah. So, I saw the messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, standing with the people on Arafah. And I said, 'By Allah, this man is from the Hums. What's he doing here?'\" Of course, this was all ended with Islam. And as we can see today, when we look at the pilgrimage, there's no Hums. And the people were equal in the designation of obligations and their clothing.",), "Farid Responds, “The Religion and Superstitions of pre-Islamic Arabs”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=66UjYhSopgM", ((335, "5:35–6:31"), (393, "6:33–8:13"), (493, "8:13–9:14"), (554, "9:14–10:30"), (632, "10:32–11:14"), (676, "11:16–13:00"), (782, "13:02–14:34"), (877, "14:37–15:59"), (961, "16:01–16:32"), (994, "16:34–17:49"), (1073, "17:53–18:36"), (1118, "18:38–20:03"), (1206, "20:06–20:48"), (1250, "20:50–21:16"), (1278, "21:18–22:38"), (1359, "22:39–23:24"),))

