#passage(("He starts by saying: OK, [prove] to me Abraham and Ishmael built the Kaaba, and give me archaeological evidence, or give me Jewish sources. So, why are we supposed to provide proof for a historical event, that specific historical event is Abraham building something, through sources like Jewish literature? Or through sources like archaeological events? Why do we need to do that? How does that prove or disprove the religion of Islam?", "I'm sure everyone heard this idea: \"absence of evidence is not evidence of absence\". Just because I don't have evidence to show specifically, without a shadow of a doubt, archaeological evidence, or from Jewish sources, that Abraham was the one who specifically built the Kaaba, therefore that is not true? That's not how it works! That's not logic! That doesn't disprove the idea. It doesn't disprove the Quranic claim. You have no evidence opposite to that. So, this is a very funny false method of argumentation.", "Do we have any evidence that Ishmael and Abraham were the ones who built the Kaaba? That is something that all the Arabs believed! That's not something that the Arabs did not know or did not believe. Even the uncle of the Prophetﷺ, Abu Talib, he has a piece of poetry called اللّامِيَّة (allaamiyya), and Abu Talib died as a Disbeliever. He was one of the leaders of Quraysh. He was the uncle of Prophet Muhammad, but he didn't accept Islam, and he died not being a Muslim. And he had a piece of poetry, allamiyya, where he refers to the building of the Kaaba, refers to it to Abraham specifically. It says: ومَوطئُ إبراهيمَ في الصَخرِ رَطبةٌ ◇ على قَدَمَيهِ حافياً غيرَ ناعلِ (And the footprint of Abraham upon the rock, with his feet barefooted not wearing something). So he talks about the footprint of Abraham, which we still have until this day, the footprint of Abraham, peace be upon him, where he was building the Kaaba. So now here, the uncle of the Prophet Muhammadﷺ, a Disbeliever of Quraysh, refers to that specific thing.", "And he says: وأَشواطٌ بَينَ المَروَتَينِ إلى الصَّفا ◇ وما فيهما من صورةٍ وتَماثِلِ. And he says: the أشواط, the places of running or pilgrimage, or the places where they used to do the practises of pilgrimage in Makka, between asSafa and alMarwa, which are the two mountains, and what is in them from idols and pictures. So he's referring to the Kaaba when it was full of idols still. So this is Pre-Islamic, and this is from Abu Talib, and he's talking to Quraysh there, the Disbelievers of Quraysh. This was a common belief among the Arabs; this is not something which is invented by Islam.", "And you know what's the funniest thing? If it's not true that Abraham and Ishmael built the Kaaba, the first thing the Arabs will say: This is a lie! Because the Arabs are the ones who lived in that region and have the history and the stories from their ancestors. And the first thing they will say: You're a liar! Actually, no, the one who built the Kaaba is this person or that person. They would instantly disprove Prophet Muhammad. Bring me one person who came to Prophet Muhammad and told him: Why does your Quran say that Abraham built the Kaaba? So, that is already by itself proof that the Arabs, historically, generation after generation, believed and knew from the start, like this poetry that I mentioned from Abu Talib, that the person who built the Kaaba was Abraham and Ishmael. And like I said, you cannot disprove this. Look at any historian that talked about the history of the Arab: all of them will agree and have consensus that the ones who built the Kaaba were Abraham and Ishmael.", "By the way, even if you look at the Torah, you have instances in Genesis, for example, Genesis:16, verse:7, which talks about Hagar and Ishmael, and it has a story that we Muslims have. We have the Well of Zamzam, which is in Makka, where the water continuously come, which we believe is a blessed well, and we have that story there where the Angel comes to Hagar, and there is a blessed well there, and it has a specific name that God gives it, and that story is in the Bible, in Genesis, and it mentions this place \"Shur\". And this place Shur is in Northern Saudi Arabia. This place Shur is in the deserts of Arabia. So they were there! And it talks about the well, the well that we believe in, the Well of Zamzam. And then also (Genesis21:21), if you go fast forward a few chapters, it talks about Hagar and Ishmael and how they lived in the Desert of Paran. The Deserts of Paran are the Deserts of Arabia, and that's according to overwhelming majority of Historical Scholars, even Non-Muslims ones, where they say Paran is the Deserts of Arabia. So, if they lived in the deserts of the Arabian Peninsula, then obviously Abraham has visited them there, and obviously, the Arabs would know about them more than anyone else. So if the Arabs generally believed that Abraham and Ishmael are the ones who built the Kaaba, and none of them ever accused the Prophet Muhammad of lying by making that claim, then this is more than historical proof that Abraham and Ishmael built the Kaaba.", "The last thing I'm going to say, to show you how flawed this method is: I'm going to apply the same method to you. In Genesis:13, verse:18, it talks about Abraham going to a specific location, in Mamre in Hebron, and then it says he pitched a tent there and he built an Altar to the Lord, near the great trees of Mamre in Hebron. We know where Hebron is today. And we know where these great trees of Mamre are. Now, give me proof that Abraham built an Altar to the Lord there. We don't even have an Altar to the Lord there! And honestly, archaeological evidence, any Pre-Jewish sources are fair game! I'm going to use the same argument to see how absurd this argumentation is. Because we know, when we go back so far to this Period in history, we know that we don't have remnants of archaeological evidence that can prove any of that. And we don't have anything other than the history of the Arabs. And the Arabs were not known to write down their history like that: Oh! Abraham did this, Abraham did that! They were not that type of a nation. Most of the Arabs at that time were illiterate. So, it's a very flawed method!",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Clip-React| PBD Calls Me An Islamist?! Five Reasons To Debunk Islam?! | Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=S1CSlMRuy9s", ((721, "12:01–12:27"), (755, "12:35–13:03"), (801, "13:21–14:13"), (860, "14:20–14:59"), (905, "15:05–16:05"), (975, "16:15–17:38"), (1061, "17:41–19:06"),))
